{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Ballast Water Invasion: A Foreign Bacterium in Long Beach Harbor",
    "date": "2026-09-28",
    "lesson": "Ships discharge millions of liters of ballast water taken on in one port and released in another, and with it, whole microbial communities hitch a ride across oceans. In Long Beach Harbor, a copiotrophic bacterium from a distant estuary is dumped into resident water alongside the native harbor community. Both must exploit the same organic pollution plume (a mix of ship-derived hydrocarbon residue, sewage-adjacent protein, and algal carbohydrate detritus) but the invader has no evolved relationship with local phages, grazers, or competitors. This models real invasion biology: success depends on whether the newcomer's metabolism matches the available food faster than resident predators and viruses can suppress it. High traffic and nutrient loading make this a crowded, high-turnover, frantic environment very different from an open-ocean level.",
    "citation": "Ruiz et al. 2000, Global spread of microorganisms by ships (Nature); ballast water invasion ecology studies, Port of Long Beach.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Ballast water discharge as a vector for microbial bioinvasion, Port of Long Beach, CA"
  },
